.TH ZEROFS 1
.SH NAME
zerofs \- a file server, in Limbo, serving one file of zeros
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {zerofs}
.I dir
.br
.B styxlisten
.I address
.B zerofs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Zerofs
serves 9P on its standard input, presenting one directory holding one
read-only file,
.BR zero ,
which returns as many zero bytes as are asked for.
.PP
It exists to make
.IR styxbench (1)'s
comparison a fair one.  That tool times a Styx round trip against a
bare TCP exchange, and its first version answered the TCP half with a
Limbo program and the Styx half with the kernel \(em
.I exportfs
reading
.B /dev/zero
in C.  The ratio it produced measured which language the server was
written in rather than the protocol, and it came out with Styx cheaper
than the transport it runs on, which cannot be so.
.PP
With
.I zerofs
on the Styx side both halves pay Dis interpretation and what is left
between them is the protocol.  Serving zeros rather than anything real
is deliberate: a read then costs the server nothing but the exchange
itself.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/zerofs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxbench (1),
.IR tcpserve (1),
.IR styxlisten (1)
